diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the inter-AS physical link on router2 (eth0) is down, which prevents the eBGP peering and inter-domain forwarding. Mechanism: with router2’s eth0 down, no L2/L3 connectivity exists on 193.10.11.0/24. Router2 cannot source or receive BGP keepalives to 193.10.11.1, so its BGP session transitions to Active and withdraws learned routes; traffic between LANs then blackholes at router1 due to failed next-hop resolution. Localization: router2 eth0 shows administratively down and no carrier (ip addr shows eth0 state DOWN with no UP/RUNNING flags; ifconfig flags 4098 <BROADCAST,MULTICAST>; ethtool reports “Link detected: no”). Router2 cannot even ping 193.10.11.1 (“Network is unreachable”), and router1 cannot reach 193.10.11.2 (“Destination Host Unreachable”). Router2’s “show bgp summary” confirms the session is down (Active). Router1 still shows a BGP-learned 200.1.1.0/24 and neighbor up ~2 minutes; that’s consistent with the failure occurring very recently and router1’s hold timer not expiring yet or stale state prior to withdrawal. Root cause: router2’s inter-AS interface eth0 was left administratively down or lost its physical link. Corrective fix: restore router2 eth0—ensure the cable/virtual link is present and bring the interface up (ip link set eth0 up). Verify carrier (ethtool shows Link detected: yes), confirm bidirectional ping on 193.10.11.x, and ensure eBGP re-establishes and routes between 195.11.14.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24 are exchanged. Consider enabling BFD for faster failure detection. 
=== END ===